# NOTES
- Fangjiashan (both units): confirmed CNNC-owned (72% CNNC stake, adjacent to the CNNC Qinshan complex), NOT CGN as the heuristic assigned. This is the same class of owner-group misattribution bug as the documented Lufeng case, but in the reverse direction (a CNNC site mislabeled CGN, vs. Lufeng being a CGN site mislabeled CNNC). "CNPEC" is an ambiguous acronym in this dataset — it can mean CNNC's engineering subsidiary (China Nuclear Power Engineering Co., the actual Fangjiashan constructor) or informally suggest CGN's own engineering arm; recommend disambiguating this acronym everywhere it appears.
- Haiyang (all 4 units): confirmed owner/operator is State Power Investment Corporation (SPIC), via its Shandong Nuclear Power subsidiary — NOT CNNC. This affects Units 3-4 (CAP1000, currently tagged CNNC/CNPE) most directly, but also means Units 1-2's "constructor=Westinghouse" field conflates the AP1000 reactor technology licensor with the actual EPC/civil contractor (CNECC per WNA).
- Systemic "constructor" field ambiguity found across multiple CNNC-heuristic sites (Fuqing, Haiyang, and likely more outside this batch): the heuristic populates "constructor" with the parent owner-group name (CNNC), but industry sources consistently name a distinct literal EPC/civil-works contractor — China Nuclear Engineering Corporation (CNECC), executed via its regional bureaus. Recommend clarifying whether the "constructor" column is meant to capture parent ownership or the literal EPC firm, and auditing all CNNC-tagged sites in the full database against this pattern (Changjiang and Fangchenggang in this batch could not be checked due to lack of unit-specific sources, so this correction was NOT applied there — flagged as UNVERIFIABLE rather than assumed).
- Fuqing turbine_supplier: the "Harbin Electric" default for Units 1-4 (M310+) is wrong — Unit 1 is directly confirmed Dongfang Electric (HIGH), and Units 5-6 (HPR1000) are directly confirmed Dongfang Electric (HIGH); Units 2-4 were corrected to Dongfang by inference from this pattern (MED/LOW, not independently unit-sourced) rather than left on the wrong Harbin default.
- Fast reactors (CEFR, CFR-600/Xiapu Units 1-2) correctly have no RPV (rpv_manufacturer=None is structurally right), but the CNNC/CNPE default for constructor/architect_engineer was wrong for CEFR specifically — CEFR has a substantial Russian design lineage (OKBM Afrikantov, OKB Gidropress, NIKIET, Kurchatov Institute) built with CIAE, not a purely domestic CNNC/CNPE project. CFR-600's constructor/AE beyond "CNNC broadly designed it" could not be sourced.
- Daya Bay architect_engineer: the compound guess "EDF / CNPEC" is likely a garbled/anachronistic entry — CNPEC (a CGN engineering subsidiary) postdates Daya Bay's 1980s construction era. Corrected to EDF alone; a further refinement to "EDF/SOFINEL" (EDF's joint engineering venture with Framatome at the time) is plausible but was not independently confirmed this session.
- Bailong Unit 1: another owner-group misattribution — actual owner/developer is SPIC (via Guangxi Nuclear Power Co.) with SNERDI as architect-engineer, not CNNC/CNPE.
- SESSION LIMITATION: the WebSearch tool budget (200/200 calls) was exhausted before this batch's research began (confirmed via a direct test query that returned a budget-exhausted message), so essentially all findings above come from WebFetch against known/guessed URLs (Wikipedia, WNA country profile, cached search snippets) rather than live search. This is a materially weaker verification path — the large UNVERIFIABLE counts for Changjiang (16/16 fields), Fangchenggang (16/16 fields), and Hongyanhe Units 2-6 (20/24 fields) likely reflect tool-access limitations more than genuine unavailability of sourcing. Recommend re-running this batch's gaps once WebSearch budget resets, prioritizing: Hongyanhe turbine suppliers (all 6 units), Fangchenggang all fields, and Changjiang all fields.
